Article 68 A legal person shall terminate upon occurrence of any of the following reasons and upon completion of liquidation and deregistration in accordance with the law:
(1) The legal person is dissolved;
(2) The legal person is declared bankrupt;
(3) Other reasons provided by the law.
Where the termination of a legal person requires approval from relevant authorities under any law or administrative regulation, such provisions shall prevail.

Article 69 A legal person shall be dissolved under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The term of existence specified in its articles of association expires or another cause of dissolution specified in its articles of association arises;
(2) Its governing body adopts a resolution to dissolve it;
(3) It needs to be dissolved due to merger or division;
(4) Its business license or registration certificate is lawfully revoked, it is ordered to be closed down, or it is abolished; or
(5) Other circumstances provided by the law.

Article 123: Civil subjects shall enjoy intellectual property rights in accordance with law.
Intellectual property rights are the exclusive rights enjoyed by the right holder in accordance with law in respect of the following subject matters:
(1) works;
(2) inventions, utility models, and designs;
(3) trademarks;
(4) geographical indications;
(5) trade secrets;
(6) integrated circuit layout designs;
(7) new plant varieties;
(8) other subject matters as provided by law.

Article 173 Under any of the following circumstances, the agency by agreement shall terminate:
(1) The term of agency expires or the agency task is completed;
(2) The principal withdraws from the appointment or the agent resigns from the appointment;
(3) The agent loses its capacity for civil conduct;
(4) The agent or the principal dies; or
(5) The legal person or unincorporated organization acting as the agent or the principal terminates.

Article 174 After the death of the principal, under any of the following circumstances, the act of agency performed by the agent by agreement is valid:
(1) The agent does not know and should not have known of the death of the principal;
(2) The heir of the principal recognizes it;
(3) It is expressly provided in the authorization that the power of agency shall terminate upon completion of the agency task; or
(4) The act was already performed before the death of the principal and the agency continues for the benefit of the heir of the principal.
Where the legal person or unincorporated organization acting as the principal terminates, the provisions of the preceding paragraph shall apply mutatis mutandis.

Article 175 Under any of the following circumstances, the agency by operation of law shall terminate:
(1) The principal acquires or recovers full capacity for civil conduct;
(2) The agent loses capacity for civil conduct;
(3) The agent or the principal dies; or
(4) Other circumstances provided for by law.

Article 179 The main methods of bearing civil liability include:
(1) cessation of infringement;
(2) removal of obstruction;
(3) elimination of danger;
(4) return of property;
(5) restoration to original condition;
(6) repair, rework, or replacement;
(7) continued performance;
(8) compensation for losses;
(9) payment of liquidated damages;
(10) elimination of adverse effects and restoration of reputation; and
(11) extension of apology.
Where the law provides for punitive damages, the provisions there shall apply.
The methods of bearing civil liability provided for in this Article may be applied individually or in combination.

Article 194 The limitation of action shall be suspended within the last six months of the limitation period if a claim cannot be exercised due to the following obstacles:
(1) force majeure;
(2) the person with no capacity for civil conduct or the person with limited capacity for civil conduct has no statutory agent, or the statutory agent dies, loses the capacity for civil conduct, or loses the power of agency;
(3) after the commencement of succession, no heir or estate administrator has been determined;
(4) the right holder is controlled by the obligor or another person; or
(5) other obstacles that prevent the right holder from exercising the claim.
The limitation of action shall expire after six months have elapsed from the date on which the cause of the suspension is eliminated.

Article 195 The limitation of action shall be interrupted under any of the following circumstances, and the limitation period shall be recalculated from the time of the interruption or the conclusion of the relevant proceeding:
(1) the right holder makes a request for performance to the obligor;
(2) the obligor agrees to perform the obligation;
(3) the right holder initiates litigation or applies for arbitration; or
(4) other circumstances having the same effect as initiating litigation or applying for arbitration.

Article 196 The following claims shall not be subject to the provisions on limitation of action:
(1) claims for cessation of infringement, removal of obstruction, or elimination of danger;
(2) claims by the right holder of a real right over immovable property or a registered movable property for return of property;
(3) claims for payment of child support, alimony, or maintenance; or
(4) other claims that are not subject to the limitation of action according to law.

Article 212 The registration authority shall perform the following duties:
(1) to examine the certificate of title and other necessary materials provided by the applicant;
(2) to inquire of the applicant about the relevant registration matters;
(3) to truthfully and promptly register the relevant matters; and
(4) other duties prescribed by law and administrative regulations.
Where the relevant circumstances of the immovable property for which registration is applied need further verification, the registration authority may require the applicant to supplement materials, and may, if necessary, conduct an on-site inspection.

Article 260 Immovable and movable property owned by a collective shall include:
(1) Land, forests, mountains, grasslands, wastelands, and tidal flats owned by the collective as provided by law;
(2) Buildings, production facilities, and irrigation and water conservancy facilities owned by the collective;
(3) Education, science, culture, health, sports, and other facilities owned by the collective; and
(4) Other immovable and movable property owned by the collective.

Article 261 The immovable and movable properties owned by a farmers' collective shall be under the collective ownership of all the members of that collective. The following matters shall be decided by the members of the collective according to the statutory procedures:
(1) plan for land contracting and the contracting of land to organizations or individuals outside the collective for operation;
(2) adjustment of contracted land among individual holders of land contract management rights;
(3) measures for the use and distribution of funds such as land compensation fees;
(4) changes in ownership, etc., of enterprises with collective investment; and
(5) other matters provided by law.

Article 278 The following matters shall be decided jointly by the owners:
(1) formulating and amending the rules of procedure of the owners' assembly;
(2) formulating and amending the management regulations;
(3) electing the owners' committee or replacing its members;
(4) selecting and dismissing property service enterprises or other managers;
(5) using maintenance funds for the building and its ancillary facilities;
(6) raising maintenance funds for the building and its ancillary facilities;
(7) altering or rebuilding the building and its ancillary facilities;
(8) changing the use of common parts or engaging in business activities by utilizing common parts; and
(9) other major matters concerning co-ownership and joint management rights.
A decision on a matter to be jointly decided by the owners shall be voted on by owners whose exclusive parts account for at least two-thirds of the total area of exclusive parts and who constitute at least two-thirds of the total number of owners. Decisions on matters specified in subparagraphs (6) through (8) of the preceding paragraph shall require the consent of owners whose exclusive parts account for at least three-fourths of the area of exclusive parts of those participating in the vote and who constitute at least three-fourths of the number of owners participating in the vote. Decisions on other matters specified in the preceding paragraph shall require the consent of owners whose exclusive parts account for more than half of the area of exclusive parts of those participating in the vote and who constitute more than half of the number of owners participating in the vote.

Article 348 Where the right to use construction land is established by means of transfer through bidding, auction, agreement, or the like, the parties shall conclude a contract on the transfer of the right to use construction land in writing.
A contract on the transfer of the right to use construction land generally includes the following clauses:
(1) the names and domiciles of the parties;
(2) the boundaries, area, etc. of the land;
(3) the space occupied by buildings, structures, and auxiliary facilities thereof;
(4) the purposes and planning conditions of the land;
(5) the term of the right to use construction land;
(6) the transfer fee and other expenses and the method of payment;
(7) the methods for dispute resolution.

Article 367 To establish a right of habitation, the parties shall conclude a contract on the right of habitation in writing.
A contract on the right of habitation generally includes the following clauses:
(1) the name or title and domicile of the parties;
(2) the location of the residence;
(3) the conditions and requirements for habitation;
(4) the term of the right of habitation; and
(5) the method for dispute resolution.

Article 373 To establish an easement, the parties shall conclude a contract on the easement in writing.
A contract on an easement generally includes the following clauses:
(1) the name or title and domicile of the parties;
(2) the location of the servient land and the dominant land;
(3) the purpose and method of use;
(4) the term of the easement;
(5) the fees and method of payment thereof; and
(6) the method for dispute resolution.

Article 384 In any of the following circumstances, the owner of the servient tenement shall have the right to rescind the easement contract, and the easement shall be extinguished:
(1) The easement is abused in violation of laws or contractual stipulations;
(2) Where the servient tenement is used for compensation, the fee is not paid after two demands within a reasonable period following the expiration of the agreed payment term.

Article 393 A security interest shall be extinguished in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The principal claim is extinguished;
(2) The security interest is realized;
(3) The creditor waives the security interest;
(4) Other circumstances provided by law for the extinction of a security interest.

Article 395 The following property over which the debtor or a third person has the right to dispose of may be mortgaged:
(1) Buildings and other things attached to the land;
(2) The right to use construction land;
(3) The right to use sea areas;
(4) Production equipment, raw materials, semi-finished products, and finished products;
(5) Buildings, ships, and aircraft under construction;
(6) Means of transport;
(7) Other property not prohibited by laws or administrative regulations from being mortgaged.
A mortgagor may mortgage all property listed in the preceding paragraph together.

Article 399 The following property shall not be mortgaged:
(1) Land ownership;
(2) The right to use collective-owned land, such as residential land, reserved land, and hilly land, unless the law provides that it may be mortgaged;
(3) Educational facilities, medical and health facilities, and other public welfare facilities owned by non-profit legal persons established for public welfare purposes, such as schools, kindergartens, and medical institutions;
(4) Property whose ownership or right to use is unclear or disputed;
(5) Property that has been sealed, seized, or placed under supervision according to law;
(6) Other property that is prohibited by provisions of laws or administrative regulations from being mortgaged.

Article 400 To create a mortgage, the parties shall conclude a mortgage contract in writing.
A mortgage contract generally includes the following clauses:
(1) The type and amount of the secured claim;
(2) The time limit for the debtor to perform the debt;
(3) The name, quantity, and other information of the mortgaged property;
(4) The scope of the security.

Article 411 Where a mortgage is created in accordance with Article 396 of this Code, the mortgaged property shall be determined upon the occurrence of any of the following circumstances:
(1) The time limit for the performance of the obligation expires and the claim is not satisfied;
(2) The mortgagor is declared bankrupt or dissolved;
(3) The circumstance agreed upon by the parties for the enforcement of the mortgage right occurs; or
(4) Any other circumstance that materially affects the satisfaction of the claim.

Article 423 The claims of the mortgagee shall be determined under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the agreed period for determining the claims expires;
(2) if no period for determining the claims is agreed or the agreement is unclear, the mortgagee or the mortgagor requests the determination of the claims two years after the establishment of the maximum mortgage right;
(3) it is impossible for new claims to arise;
(4) the mortgagee knows or should know that the mortgaged property has been sealed up or distrained;
(5) the debtor or the mortgagor is declared bankrupt or dissolved; or
(6) other circumstances under which the claims are determined as prescribed by law.

Article 427 To create a pledge right, the parties shall enter into a pledge contract in writing.
A pledge contract generally includes the following clauses:
(1) the type and amount of the secured claim;
(2) the time limit for the debtor to perform the obligation;
(3) the name, quantity, and other information of the pledged property;
(4) the scope of the security; and
(5) the time and method of delivery of the pledged property.

Article 440 The following rights that the debtor or a third party has the right to dispose of may be pledged:
(1) drafts, promissory notes, and checks;
(2) bonds and certificates of deposit;
(3) warehouse receipts and bills of lading;
(4) transferable fund units and equities;
(5) property rights in intellectual property such as transferable rights to exclusively use a registered trademark, patent rights, and copyrights;
(6) existing and future accounts receivable; and
(7) other property rights that may be pledged as prescribed by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 470 The content of a contract shall be agreed upon by the parties and generally includes the following clauses:
(1) Names or titles and domiciles of the parties;
(2) Object;
(3) Quantity;
(4) Quality;
(5) Price or remuneration;
(6) Time, place, and method of performance;
(7) Liability for breach of contract; and
(8) Methods for dispute resolution.
The parties may conclude a contract by reference to model texts for various types of contracts.

Article 478 An offer ceases to be effective under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The offer is rejected;
(2) The offer is lawfully revoked;
(3) The time limit for acceptance expires and the offeree has not made an acceptance; or
(4) The offeree materially alters the content of the offer.

Article 545 A creditor may transfer all or part of its claim to a third party, except in any of the following circumstances:
(1) the claim is non-transferable by its nature;
(2) the claim is non-transferable according to the agreement of the parties;
(3) the claim is non-transferable according to the provisions of laws.
Where the parties agree that a non-monetary claim is non-transferable, such agreement shall not be asserted against a bona fide third party. Where the parties agree that a monetary claim is non-transferable, such agreement shall not be asserted against a third party.

Article 549 The debtor may plead set-off against the assignee in any of the following circumstances:
(1) at the time the debtor receives the notice of the assignment of the claim, the debtor holds a claim against the assignor, and the debtor's claim falls due before or at the same time as the assigned claim;
(2) the debtor's claim and the assigned claim arise from the same contract.

Article 557 A claim or debt shall be terminated in any of the following circumstances:
(1) the debt has been performed;
(2) the debts are set off against each other;
(3) the debtor has duly deposited the subject matter;
(4) the creditor has released the debtor from liability;
(5) the claim and the debt merge in the same person;
(6) other circumstances under which termination is provided for by law or agreed upon by the parties.
Where a contract is rescinded, the rights and obligations under the contract shall be terminated.

Article 563 A party may rescind the contract under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the purpose of the contract cannot be achieved due to force majeure;
(2) before the expiration of the period for performance, the other party expressly states, or indicates by its conduct, that it will not perform the principal obligation;
(3) the other party delays the performance of the principal obligation, and after being demanded, still fails to perform within a reasonable time;
(4) the other party delays the performance of the obligation or commits another breach of contract, rendering the purpose of the contract unattainable; or
(5) other circumstances provided by law.
For an open-ended contract with continuous performance obligations, a party may rescind the contract at any time, provided it gives the other party reasonable advance notice.

Article 570 Under any of the following circumstances, where it is difficult to perform the debt, the debtor may deposit the subject matter:
(1) the creditor refuses to accept the performance without justified reason;
(2) the whereabouts of the creditor are unknown;
(3) the creditor dies without ascertaining its heir or estate administrator, or loses capacity for civil conduct without ascertaining its guardian; or
(4) other circumstances provided by law.
If the subject matter is not suitable for deposit or the cost of deposit is too high, the debtor may, in accordance with law, auction or sell the subject matter and deposit the proceeds.

Article 687 Where the parties agree in the suretyship contract that the surety shall bear suretyship liability when the debtor is unable to perform the obligation, this is a general suretyship. 
The surety in a general suretyship has the right to refuse to bear suretyship liability vis-à-vis the creditor before the dispute on the principal contract is adjudicated or arbitrated and the debtor's property is still insufficient to perform the obligation after compulsory enforcement according to law, except in any of the following circumstances: 
(1) the debtor's whereabouts are unknown and there is no property available for enforcement; 
(2) the People's Court has accepted a bankruptcy case of the debtor; 
(3) the creditor has evidence proving that the debtor's property is insufficient to perform all the obligations or the debtor has lost the capacity to perform the obligation; or 
(4) the surety has waived the right provided in this paragraph in writing.

Article 724 Under any of the following circumstances, where the leased property cannot be used for reasons not attributable to the lessee, the lessee may dissolve the contract:
(1) the leased property is lawfully sealed up or seized by a judicial organ or an administrative organ;
(2) the ownership of the leased property is disputed;
(3) the leased property violates the compulsory provisions of laws or administrative regulations regarding conditions of use.

Article 748 The lessor shall guarantee the lessee's possession and use of the leased object.
The lessee shall have the right to request the lessor to compensate for losses in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The lessor takes back the leased object without just cause;
(2) The lessor hinders or interferes with the lessee's possession and use of the leased object without just cause;
(3) A third party claims rights over the leased object due to the lessor's reasons; or
(4) Other circumstances that improperly affect the lessee's possession and use of the leased object.

Article 909 The depositary shall sign or affix its seal on the warehouse receipt. A warehouse receipt shall include the following:
(1) The name and domicile of the depositor;
(2) The type, quantity, quality, packaging, number of items, and markings of the stored goods;
(3) The standard for loss or spoilage of the stored goods;
(4) The storage premises;
(5) The storage period;
(6) The warehousing fee;
(7) If the stored goods have been insured, the insurance amount, period, and name of the insurer; and
(8) The issuer, place of issue, and date of issue.

Article 985 Where an enriched person obtains unjust benefits without legal basis, the aggrieved person may request the enriched person to return the benefits obtained, except under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the performance is made to fulfill a moral obligation;
(2) the payment is made before the debt becomes due;
(3) the debt settlement is made with the knowledge that there is no obligation to perform.

Article 1020: The following acts may be reasonably carried out without the consent of the portrait right holder:
(1) using a portrait already disclosed by the portrait right holder within the necessary scope for personal learning, artistic appreciation, classroom teaching, or scientific research;
(2) inevitably creating, using, or disclosing the portrait of the portrait right holder for the purpose of implementing news reporting;
(3) creating, using, or disclosing the portrait of the portrait right holder within the necessary scope by state organs for the purpose of performing their duties in accordance with the law;
(4) inevitably creating, using, or disclosing the portrait of the portrait right holder for the purpose of displaying a specific public environment; and
(5) other acts of creating, using, or disclosing the portrait of the portrait right holder for the purpose of safeguarding the public interest or the lawful rights and interests of the portrait right holder.

Article 1026 The determination of whether an actor has fulfilled the duty of reasonable verification as specified in item (2) of the preceding article shall take into account the following factors:
(1) The credibility of the source of the content;
(2) Whether a necessary investigation has been conducted into content that is apparently likely to provoke controversy;
(3) The timeliness of the content;
(4) The relevance of the content to public order and good customs;
(5) The likelihood of harm to the victim's reputation; and
(6) The capability and cost of verification.

Article 1033 Unless otherwise provided by law or expressly consented to by the right holder, no organization or individual may carry out any of the following acts:
(1) Intruding upon the private life of another person by means of telephone calls, text messages, instant messaging tools, emails, leaflets, or the like;
(2) Entering, filming, or peeping into private spaces such as the residence or hotel room of another person;
(3) Filming, peeping, eavesdropping on, or disclosing the private activities of another person;
(4) Filming or peeping at the private body parts of another person;
(5) Processing the private information of another person; or
(6) Infringing upon the right to privacy of another person by other means.

Article 1035 When processing personal information, the principles of lawfulness, legitimacy, and necessity shall be followed, and excessive processing shall not be allowed, and the following conditions shall be met:
(1) Obtaining the consent of the natural person or his or her guardian, unless otherwise provided by law or administrative regulations;
(2) Disclosing the rules for processing the information;
(3) Clearly indicating the purpose, method, and scope of processing the information; and
(4) Not violating the provisions of laws or administrative regulations or the agreement of both parties.
The processing of personal information includes the collection, storage, use, processing, transmission, provision, and disclosure of personal information, among others.

Article 1062 The following property acquired by husband and wife during the existence of the marital relationship constitutes community property and shall belong to both husband and wife:
(1) wages, bonuses and remuneration for labor services;
(2) income from production, business operation and investment;
(3) income from intellectual property;
(4) property inherited or donated, except those specified under Item (3) of Article 1063 of this Code; and
(5) other property that shall be owned in common.
Husband and wife shall enjoy equal right to dispose of the community property.

Article 1063 The following property shall be the separate property of one spouse:
(1) premarital property of one spouse;
(2) compensation or damages obtained by one spouse for personal injury;
(3) property which is established in a will or gift contract to belong to only one spouse;
(4) articles exclusively used by one spouse; and
(5) other property that shall belong to one spouse.

Article 1079 If one spouse alone desires a divorce, the relevant organization may mediate the matter, or such spouse may directly bring a divorce action in the People’s Court.
In hearing divorce cases, the People’s Court shall carry out mediation; if the relationship between the husband and wife indeed breaks down and mediation proves ineffective, divorce shall be granted.
Where mediation proves ineffective under any of the following circumstances, divorce shall be granted:
(1) bigamy or cohabitation of a spouse with another person;
(2) domestic violence or maltreatment or desertion of a family member;
(3) persistent bad habits such as gambling or drug addiction despite repeated remonstration;
(4) living apart for at least two years due to discord; or
(5) any other circumstance causing the breakdown of the relationship between husband and wife.
If one party is declared missing and the other party brings a divorce action, divorce shall be granted.
If the People’s Court made a judgment dismissing a divorce action and the husband and wife have lived apart for another year, and one party again brings a divorce action, divorce shall be granted.

Article 1091: If divorce is caused by any of the following circumstances, the innocent party shall have the right to claim damages:
(1) bigamy;
(2) cohabitation with another person;
(3) committing domestic violence;
(4) maltreating or abandoning family members;
(5) having other major faults.

Article 1093: The following minors may be adopted:
(1) orphans who have lost their parents;
(2) minors whose biological parents cannot be found;
(3) children whose biological parents have special difficulties and are unable to raise them.

Article 1094: The following individuals or organizations may act as persons placing out children for adoption:
(1) guardians of an orphan;
(2) children's welfare institutions;
(3) biological parents who have special difficulties and are unable to raise their children.

Article 1098: An adoptive parent shall simultaneously satisfy the following conditions:
(1) having no children or only one child;
(2) having the ability to raise, educate, and protect the adoptee;
(3) not suffering from any disease medically deemed unsuitable for adopting a child;
(4) having no criminal record that is detrimental to the healthy growth of the adoptee;
(5) being at least thirty years old.

Article 1125: A successor shall be disinherited upon committing any of the following acts:
(1) intentional killing of the decedent;
(2) killing of another successor for the purpose of competing for the estate;
(3) abandonment of the decedent, or maltreatment of the decedent under grave circumstances;
(4) forgery, alteration, concealment, or destruction of the will under grave circumstances; or
(5) using fraudulent or coercive means to compel or prevent the decedent from making, altering, or revoking a will under grave circumstances.
Where a successor commits any of the acts specified in subparagraphs (3) through (5) of the preceding paragraph but shows genuine repentance and the decedent expresses forgiveness or subsequently names the successor in the will as a successor, such successor shall not be disinherited.
A legatee who commits any of the acts specified in the first paragraph shall lose the right to receive the legacy.

Article 1140: The following persons shall not act as witnesses to a will:
(1) persons without capacity for civil conduct, persons with limited capacity for civil conduct, and other persons without the capacity to witness;
(2) successors and legatees;
(3) persons having an interest in the successors or legatees.

Article 1147 The estate administrator shall perform the following duties:
(1) to inventory the estate and prepare a list thereof;
(2) to report on the estate to the heirs;
(3) to take necessary measures to prevent the estate from being damaged or lost;
(4) to handle the claims and debts of the deceased;
(5) to distribute the estate in accordance with the will or in accordance with the law; and
(6) to carry out other necessary actions related to the administration of the estate.

Article 1154 Under any of the following circumstances, the relevant part of the estate shall be dealt with in accordance with statutory succession:
(1) the testamentary heir renounces the succession or the legatee renounces the legacy;
(2) the testamentary heir loses the right of succession or the legatee loses the right to legacy;
(3) the testamentary heir or the legatee predeceases the testator or terminates;
(4) the part of the estate affected by the void part of the will; and
(5) the part of the estate not disposed of by the will.

Article 1235 Where damage is caused to the ecological environment in violation of state regulations, the authority designated by the state or the organization provided for by law shall have the right to request the tortfeasor to compensate for the following losses and expenses:
(1) Losses resulting from the loss of service function during the period from the occurrence of the ecological environmental damage to its remedy;
(2) Losses caused by permanent damage to the ecological environment function;
(3) Expenses for investigation, identification, and assessment of the ecological environmental damage;
(4) Expenses for pollution removal and ecological environmental remediation; and
(5) Reasonable expenses incurred to prevent the occurrence and spread of the damage.
